This problem involves finding the lexicographically smallest path of length k in a grid. The approach is to use depth-first search (DFS) to generate all possible paths of length k and then select the lexicographically smallest one.

Here is a Java solution using DFS:

```java
public List<Integer> minPath(List<List<Integer>> grid, int k) {
    int n = grid.size();
    boolean[][] visited = new boolean[n][n];
    List<Integer> minPath = new ArrayList<>();
    List<List<Integer>> allPaths = new ArrayList<>();

    // recursive function to generate all paths
    dfs(grid, visited, new ArrayList<>(), allPaths, 0, 0, k);

    // find the lexicographically smallest path
    for (List<Integer> path : allPaths) {
        if (minPath.isEmpty() || comparePaths(path, minPath) < 0) {
            minPath = new ArrayList<>(path);
        }
    }

    return minPath;
}

// helper function to compare two paths
private int comparePaths(List<Integer> path1, List<Integer> path2) {
    int size = Math.min(path1.size(), path2.size());
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        int cmp = Integer.compare(path1.get(i), path2.get(i));
        if (cmp != 0) {
            return cmp;
        }
    }
    return Integer.compare(path1.size(), path2.size());
}

// recursive function to generate all paths
private void dfs(List<List<Integer>> grid, boolean[][] visited, List<Integer> curPath, List<List<Integer>> allPaths, int i, int j, int k) {
    if (k == 0) {
        allPaths.add(new ArrayList<>(curPath));
        return;
    }
    if (i < 0 || i >= grid.size() || j < 0 || j >= grid.size() || visited[i][j]) {
        return;
    }
    visited[i][j] = true;
    curPath.add(grid.get(i).get(j));
    dfs(grid, visited, curPath, allPaths, i + 1, j, k - 1);
    dfs(grid, visited, curPath, allPaths, i - 1, j, k - 1);
    dfs(grid, visited, curPath, allPaths, i, j + 1, k - 1);
    dfs(grid, visited, curPath, allPaths, i, j - 1, k - 1);
    curPath.remove(curPath.size() - 1);
    visited[i][j] = false;
}
```

This solution first generates all possible paths of length k using DFS. Then, it compares each path with the current smallest path and updates the smallest path accordingly. The time complexity of this solution is O((N^2)^k), which is not efficient for large inputs. A more efficient solution would be to use a priority queue to keep track of the smallest k paths as they are generated.

